A||||12||CUTTER||Big Trany Prob....HELP!!!!||||13||
Z||000000||CUTTER||12-21-1999||10:25 PM||||Well...I have a HUDGE prob. with my trany. I have a auto 87GT. In 1st gear its fine, but it just wont shift in 2nd. When Im in drive and take off it redlines no matter how fast I get up to speed and wont shift into 2nd. After slamming the peddle it finaly shifts...but into 3rd. Im pretty sure its from neutral droping it alot when I 1st got the car and found out that it does hudge damage. I stoped neutral droping I think a little to late. Is there any other way to get it fixed without getting a new/rebuilt trans? Is it a big problem like I think it is? and how exspensive will it be too fix? 3rd gear is fine 1st slips just the slightest bit and 2nd.......huh....well lets just say I have no 2nd gear. Oh also when im in 3rd and im slowing down, I put the automatic shifter knob into 2nd. But its like im in neutral. I can hit the gas and it just revs. Any help will be GREATLY appreciated.....||||reg||13
Z||000001||Carrolles||12-22-1999||07:01 AM||||Before you spend the $ and effort replacing/rebuilding the trans you might try some of the over the counter transmission fluid additives.  My daughter got smacked in the left front fender in an 86 Pontiac 6000 with the 2.8L engine and auto.  After the car got out of the shop it started doing similar things to your trans.  Aamco said it need a new trans and that the fluid was all burned up.  I had just changed out the fluid and filter and knew better than to believe them.  A local repair shop suggested putting the additive in.  I got some from Autozone and after a few days the problem went away.  I sold the car 20000 miles later with no more trans problems.<P>Apparently when the car got hit it shook a lot of junk loose in the trans that circulated with the fluid and stuck some of the valve body parts.<P>You don't have much to lose if you try this first.  I believe Theogre tried this on his a while back with similar results.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||CUTTER||12-22-1999||03:55 PM||||I put some of that Transmition Honey in that I got at National Auto. I hope it will help, but I have my doughts||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||12-22-1999||05:56 PM||||Cutter, <P>I am not getting on your case, but it sounds as if you still abuse your car.  When you lose a gear, the smart thing to do is stop and see if you can fix it.  By driving around at redline and causing it to "jump" second gear you have probably ruined it where a simple shift kit would have repaired your problem.  The loss of second would not likely be contributed to neutral dropping.  However since SEVERE damage has already been done to your First gear, perhaps you should consider finding a rebuilt/used transmission.  Otherwise, go ahead and buy the shift kit.  I don't know offhand where you can get one for the TH-125, but for common transmissions such as the T-400 they are readily available at any neighborhood automotive supply.  I hope that this works out the best for you and perhaps that you begin to respect your car and treat it as it deserves.... In case you are wondering... No, I am not old (22)... I just like most cars I meet better than their owners...<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000004||CUTTER||12-22-1999||06:30 PM||||Well, I am a racing type of person and love to race. this is my first car and didnt know anything about transmitions or anything when I first bought it. Thats why I didnt think neutral droping wasnt bad, but then found out how bad it was and stoped doing it, but then by that time it was too late. So I guess you can call my car a "beater" My next car though will not be beatin, It will be raced, but not beatin!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||12-22-1999||06:50 PM||||You can get a shift kit through the Fiero Store for about $50US.  Although I won't pretend to know anything about transmissions (anybody know what the "R" stands for?  "Racing", maybe?), it would make sense that, if 2nd gear is gone, the rest of the tranny can't be far behind.  Get another tranny and just cruise. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||william_thorniii||12-22-1999||07:00 PM||||Cutter... <BR>I was not flaming you, but it only takes common sense.  Let's put it in simple terms.  Pretend you have two wheels that must touch to achieve a desired result.  Does it make sense to you that one should be spinning as fast as possible before they are mated?  Of course not, I am sure that you realized when neutral dropping that your transmission made an awful sound when you got it in gear... Unfortunately in race applications, I assume that you wanted to bet going as fast as possible.  So you have to find out if this racing is worth buying another trans/car.  I suggest not racing in an automatic, or if you must install a high stall torque converter.  I hope that the shift kit fixes it for you, otherwise this will be a very costly lesson.  You might want to try B&M's website or Summit/Jegs first.  FYI, a shiftkit will create quicker shifts, thereby increasing your advantage in racing applications.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000007||GREENDANO||12-25-1999||04:52 PM||||Shift kits are on sale at the Fiero store for $34.95  Plus it includes a filter kit as well.  This is a very good deal, cheapest around.  Part #54900,  I'm ordering one this coming week.<BR>  Their phone number is 1-800-343-7648<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||10
Z||000008||theogre||12-26-1999||02:07 PM||||It is extremely unlikely a shift kit will fix a broken tranny.<P>It's posible it's dirt in a passage, using a cleaner sometimes clears that. Don't hold your breath. In your case it's just as likely you blew a piston, band, or clutch pack.||||reg||1
Z||000009||dogtired||12-27-1999||02:25 AM||||Hay, I have gone through a few trannys myself, I don't mean repairing, I mean from the ram jam, slap shift side.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>So I kinda understand where you are comming from? A thought, might be to look for a replacement, at one of the local yards or look for a donar car and slip a 4 or 5 speed in it? There are several options here and I know they all have $$$ attached, but I don't think an overhaul in a can will do anything except make you $4.95 poorer!<BR>Good Luck, and Hang in there!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000010||Shiner||12-27-1999||03:30 AM||||Dogtired,<BR> For some reason, I don't think this guy is going to have more luck with a manual trans....||||reg||9
Z||000011||dogtired||12-27-1999||06:30 AM||||I believe you are absolutely correct, But he would certanily have lots more FUN and a reason to shift!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Thats what these little cars are all about FUN FUN FUN<BR>Lets us know CUTTER, what are you up to?||||reg||6
Z||000012||GREENDANO||12-27-1999||10:43 PM||||For the record, I agree a shift kit was not the answer to fixing the trans.  I posted the info for all who were interested in getting a shift kit. <BR>  I don't think a manual trans is the answer, I think the answer is knowledge, and respect for you equiptment.  Before you do anything questionable, ask yourself how much money is this going to cost me if I break it.  When you side-step a clutch with a manual trans, eventually it WILL break something.  <BR>  I know I'm not OGRE, I'm just Dan-O with 15 years expirience fixing cars, only four with Fieros, though.  So don't listen to me.<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
